Two templates, one path, and the binding that follows.

```csharp
app.MapGet("/tickets/{id}", (string id) => Results.Ok(Load(id)));
app.MapGet("/tickets/new", () => Results.Ok(BlankForm()));

// GET /tickets/new  -> the literal route wins, despite being registered second
// GET /tickets/42   -> the parameter route, with id bound to "42"
```

And the binding trap, in the form it usually appears:

```csharp
// Silently plausible: no way to tell "page 0" from "nothing sent".
app.MapGet("/tickets", (int page, int size) => Results.Ok(Page(page, size)));

// Honest: absence is representable, and the handler decides what it means.
app.MapGet("/tickets", (int? page, int? size) =>
{
    var p = page ?? 1;
    var s = size ?? 20;
    if (s is < 1 or > 100)
    {
        return Results.ValidationProblem(new Dictionary<string, string[]>
        {
            ["size"] = ["size must be between 1 and 100"]
        });
    }
    return Results.Ok(Page(p, s));
});
```

The second version is longer and states three things the first left to chance: what a missing page means, what a missing size means, and what an out-of-range size means. Note that `size = 0` from the first version would reach the paging code and probably return nothing at all, which reads in production as "the list is empty" rather than as a bad request.

